Chapter: WebChainTest
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Encode chapter TrustAnchors
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A live certificate chain from www.digicert.com, walked to a root Codex
 ships, with the hostname checked.

 This is the whole claim end to end and the first time it has been
 possible: a certificate we did not mint, an issuer we did not run, a
 chain we did not construct, verified against an anchor we chose
 deliberately and shipped ourselves -- and refused when anything about it
 is wrong.

 THE CLOCK IS FIXED ON PURPOSE. x509-verify-peer takes the current time
 from its caller, and this test hands it 2026-08-01, a date inside the
 leaf's validity window (2026-07-10 to 2026-08-25). Passing the real time
 would make the test start failing on a date, having found no defect --
 the leaf is a normal short-lived web certificate and will expire. When
 that happens this test still passes, because what it asks is whether the
 signatures and the names are right, and neither of those expires. The
 expiry check itself is exercised by the negative below, which moves the
 clock rather than the certificate.

 We say:

